The bug is the closure capturing the loop variable `i` by reference. In Go 1.21, the `for` loop variable `i` is a single variable reused on each iteration. Your goroutines do not receive a copy of the value; they all print `i` later, after the loop has finished and `i` has become `5`.

Fix it by passing `i` into each goroutine as an argument, so each goroutine gets its own value.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			fmt.Println(i)
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

