message.content:
The main bug is that `max_val` is being used for two different things: the “best value so far” and the “value of the current streak.” Because of that, this line:

```rust
if data[i] == max_val {
```

compares the current element to the best value seen so far, not to the value of the current run. After the first run of `2`s, `max_val` is set to `2`, so the following `1`s are never accumulated as a streak.

There is also a second bug: the final streak is never checked after the loop. If the longest streak ends at the last element, `max_count` is never updated for it.

The fix is to track the current streak value separately from the best streak value, compare against that current streak value, and check the final streak after the loop.

This corrected version assumes non-empty input, like the original code does.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut current_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == current_val {
            current_count += 1;
        } else {
            if current_count > max_count {
                max_count = current_count;
                max_val = current_val;
            }
            current_val = data[i];
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = current_val;
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

